Custom Transformer for Sentiment Analysis — IMDB

Date: September 19, 2026
Platform / Environment: AWS Future AI Programmer Nanodegree — PyTorch

What I Did

Completed the final project in the AWS Future AI Programmer Nanodegree.

I built and trained a custom PyTorch Transformer from scratch for binary sentiment classification using the IMDB movie review dataset.

The project adapted a causal Transformer architecture for classification by allowing tokens to attend to the full sequence rather than restricting attention to previous tokens.

Model Architecture

The model included:

* Subword tokenisation using bert-base-uncased
* Token and positional embeddings
* Bidirectional self-attention without causal masking
* Stacked Transformer blocks
* Layer normalisation
* Global average pooling using x.mean(dim=1)
* A classification head for positive/negative sentiment

Instead of using a dedicated [CLS] token, global average pooling was used to produce a single representation of the sequence.

Training

The model was trained for 6 epochs using:

* AdamW optimiser
* Cross-Entropy loss
* PyTorch
* IMDB movie review dataset

Results

* Final test accuracy: 76.02%
* Final validation accuracy: 78.40%
* Project target: >75% test accuracy

The final test accuracy exceeded the project’s required target.

What I Learned

This project helped me connect several concepts I had previously studied separately.

I now have practical experience with the basic structure of a Transformer model, including embeddings, self-attention, Transformer blocks, normalisation and classification.

One particularly useful concept was understanding the difference between causal and bidirectional attention. A causal language model restricts each token from attending to future tokens, whereas this classification model was designed so tokens could use information from the full review.

I also learned how global average pooling can turn a sequence of token representations into a single representation that can be passed to a classification head.

Key Takeaway

Building the Transformer myself made the architecture much less abstract.

Rather than only learning that Transformers contain attention and feedforward layers, I had to work with those components as part of an actual training pipeline and evaluate whether the resulting model met the required target.

Practical Relevance

This project gives me a concrete example of applying PyTorch and Transformer concepts to a real NLP classification task.

It also gives me a foundation for understanding how more advanced Transformer-based models are constructed and adapted for different tasks.

